 { White's king is badly placed, but fortunately, so is the black rook. As long as White anticipates the opponent's intentions, he can draw. } 1. { -KEY- } Re2 $1 ( { Preventing the lethal threat of } 1. Re2 Kf2 ) 
Kh3 2. Ra2 { A waiting move. } ( { a) Both } 2. Re1 $2 { (liberating the black king) } Kg2 { heading for f2(f1). } ) 
 ( { b) And } 2. Kh5 $2 { (displacing White's own king) } Rg3 $1 3. Ra2 ( { or } 3. Kh6 Kg4 { and 4...Kf3; } ) 
Rg2 4. Ra3 Re2 5. Kg5 Kg3 { followed by 6...Kf3 and 7...Rb2, were losing for White. } ) 
Rf2 ( { a) Or } 2... Rf8 3. Re2 Re8 4. Kf4 $1 ) 
 ( { b) In contrast, after } 2... Rf2 { the threat of 3...e2 must be parried, and for that there is only one move. } ) 
3. Ra3 $1 Rf3 ( { Or } 3... Re2 4. Kf4 { The text creates the threat of 4...Kg2, explaining White's reply. } ) 
4. Ra2 $1 Kg3 5. Re2 $1 { Neutralizing the threat of 5...Rf2, and reverting to the initial position. Draw. } 
