 { With an extra pawn and a more active rook, White should be winning. Nevertheless, his task was facilitated by the next, typical move. } 1. { -KEY- } Rb5 $1 { Tying the black rook to the a5-pawn. } Kg7 2. g4 Kg6 3. e3 Kf6 4. Kf3 Ra7 { Black can only wait, while White advance his positions on the kingside step by step. } 5. h3 ( { The more straightforward } 5. h4 { was, of course, also possible, as observed by darklord64. In fact, several of the upcoming moves from White's are interchangeable, and a few, such as 7.Rc5, could even be done without. } ) 
Ra8 6. Kg3 Kg6 7. Rc5 Kf6 8. h4 Kg6 9. Rb5 Kf6 10. f4 Ra7 11. Kf3 Kg6 12. Rc5 Kf6 13. Ke4 $1 { The king gets ready to move over to the queenside. } Ra8 14. Kd4 Ke7 ( { On } 14... Ra7 { White could indeed play } 15. Kc4 { heading for b5. } ) 
15. Ke5 $1 ( { By means of the threat } 16. Rc7 { Vallejo provokes ...f7-f6 in order to weaken the seventh rank and the black kingside pawns. } ) 
 ( { a) Less technical was } 15. Rh5 Kd6 16. Rxh6 $6 Rb8 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 15. Kc4 Kd6 16. Kb5 $4 Rb8 ) 
f6+ ( { Or } 15... Ra7 16. g5 { with zugzwang. } ) 
16. Ke4 Kf7 17. Rc7+ Kg8 ( { Obviously, } 17... Kg6 { failed to } 18. h5 ) 
18. Re7 Ra6 19. f5 $1 exf5+ ( { If } 19... e5 { then } 20. Re6 $1 { - De Dovitiis. } ) 
20. Kxf5 Rc6 21. Kg6 
 1-0

