5. Kf5 Bc1 6. Ba6 Bd2 7. Ke5 Ke7 8. Bc4 Bc1 9. g5 Bd2 10. Kf5 ( { After } 10. h6 gxh6 11. gxh6 Bc3 12. Kf5 Bb2 { Black manages to control both passed pawns; e.g. } 13. Kg6 Bc3 14. Kh7 Kf8 $1 { etc. } ) 
Kf8 11. a4 Bc1 12. Bb5 Bd2 13. Ke5 Ke7 14. Ba6 Bc1 15. Ke4 Bd2 16. Kf3 Bc1 17. Kg4 Bd2 18. f5 Bc3 $1 { Having completed its duty on the c1-h6 diagonal, the bishop returns to the long diagonal in order to control the crucial f6-square. } ( { Also possible was } 18... Kd6 19. h6 gxh6 20. g6 Bc3 21. Kh5 Bg7 { when } 22. f6 Bxf6 23. Kxh6 Kc5 24. g7 Bxg7 25. Kxg7 Kb4 26. Bb5 a6 27. Be8 b5 { forces a draw. Nevertheless, the text is much simpler and, as we will see next, it would draw even if Black had a pawn less on the queenside. } ) 
19. Bc4 Kf8 $1 ( { But not } 19... Bd4 $2 20. f6 $1 gxf6 21. g6 f5 22. Kxf5 Bg7 23. Kg5 Bf6 24. Kh6 Kf8 25. Kh7 { and White wins. } ) 
20. Kf3 ( { After } 20. h6 gxh6 21. gxh6 Ke7 { Black successfully applies the idea of one single diagonal, just as in the 10.h6 subline. } ) 
Bd2 21. Kg4 Bc3 22. Bd3 Bb2 23. f6 { This advance makes little sense, but White had no method of realizing his advantage anyway. } gxf6 24. g6 Kg7 25. Kf5 Bc1 
