 { White has a long-term asset in his more mobile pawn majority. Shaked now makes an interesting choice, with echoes of the famous game Em.Lasker-J.R.Capablanca, St. Petersburg 1914. } 1. { -KEY- } f5 $1 { Gaining some space but, more importantly, restricting Black's minor pieces. Incidentally, the idea of limiting a bishop by placing one's pawns on squares of the same colour is familiar to us from Example 373 (Larsen-Penrose). } ( { In contrast, } 1. Rf2 $6 f5 $1 2. e5 Be6 { yields Black freer play than in the game, despite the fact that his kingside pawns are fixed on the 'wrong' colour. } ) 
c5 { Logically enough, Benjamin prepares ...Nc6-e5 in order to exploit the fresh weakness on e5. } 2. Rf2 Nc6 3. Nd5 R2h6 4. Kf4 $1 { The squares vacated by a pawn advance are often of immediate interest to its fellow pieces, a fact that I highlighted several times in 'Mastering Chess Strategy'. Here White aims at further activating his king (what a contrast with its black counterpart!) and clearing the e3-g4 route for the knight. } Kb8 ( { Or } 4... Nd4 5. c3 Nxe2 6. Rxe2 { keeping a pleasant advantage. } ) 
5. Ne3 Ne5 6. Ng4 $1 { Swapping the prominent knight on e5, after which an advance of the pawn majority will be easier to achieve. } Nxg4 7. Bxg4 Bb7 8. Rd2 Bc6 9. Bf3 Kb7 10. g4 $1 { White is finally ready to convert his pawn majority into a passed pawn. } Rh2 $6 { This exchange merely seems to reduce Black's potential for counterplay. } ( { a) I guess that either } 10... Rh4 { interfering with the enemy plan. } ) 
 ( { b) Or } 10... a5 { creating some play on the queenside, was more promising. } ) 
11. Rxh2 Rxh2 12. Rg2 Rh1 13. g5 $1 fxg5+ ( { Obviously, } 13... Rh4 { is met by } 14. Rg4 ) 
14. Kxg5 Rf1 ( { Otherwise White plays } 14... Rf1 14. Kf6 ) 
15. Kf4 Rb1 16. b3 Rb2 $6 { This won't end well, but Black's position was already difficult. } 17. e5 $1 { Of course; White exploits the absence of Black's king in the defence. } Rxa2 18. e6 fxe6 19. f6 $1 Bxf3 20. Kxf3 e5 21. f7 
 1-0

