 { The king is a common object for the restrictive measures, as we saw, for example, in the section on cutting off the enemy king in Chapter Three. Here are two further examples.In this tense position, Black is preparing ...g7-g6 and ...Kg7 in order to liberate his knight and king. Mikhalevski finds a surprising antidote. } 1. { -KEY- } g6 $1 fxg6 ( { a) Obviously, } 1... Nxg6 $2 { loses a piece to } 2. Rc8 ) 
 ( { b) While } 1... hxg6 2. f4 $1 { is similar to the game; e.g. } b4 3. b3 f6 4. Kd2 f5 5. Ke3 g5 6. fxg5 f4 7. Kf3 g6 8. h4 $1 { with a decisive zugzwang - Mikhalevski. } ) 
2. f4 $1 { Further restricting the enemy kingside. Now it becomes clear that Black won't be able to move either his king or his minor pieces in the near future. } h5 3. Kd2 h4 4. Ke3 h3 5. b3 b4 ( { Or } 5... a4 6. bxa4 bxa4 7. a3 { with zugzwang - Mikhalevski. } ) 
6. Kf3 $1 { Black has run out of moves, and must now rely on a sacrifice. } Ne6 7. Rc8+ Kf7 8. Rxa8 Nxd4+ 9. Kg4 Ne2 10. Rxa5 { The rest is rather easy, with the rook clearly outstripping the knight. } Nc3 11. Ra7+ Ke6 12. Ra6+ $1 { A useful intermediate check to keep Black's king cut off from the sixth rank. } Ke7 13. Kf3 d4 14. Rxg6 Nxa2 15. Ke4 Kf7 16. Rb6 $1 { Yet another typical restriction measure. } Nc1 17. Rxb4 d3 18. Ke3 Kg6 19. Kd2 Ne2 20. Kxd3 Ng3 21. hxg3 h2 22. f5+ $1 ( { With } 23. Rh4 { coming up, Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

