 { Material is equal, but White's activity is overwhelming. Seeing that Black might be about to play ...Kh7-g6, Psakhis takes restrictive measures. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 Kh7 ( { After } 1... g6 $6 { , Black's cut-off king soon becomes a decisive factor; e.g. } 2. b4 Ra3 3. g4 $1 Rb3 ( { or } 3... a5 4. b5 Ra4 5. Kxe5 Rxg4 6. b6 { and the b-pawn decides - Psakhis } ) 
4. h5 $5 { (it isn't yet necessary to swap the e-pawns) } gxh5 5. gxh5 a5 6. b5 a4 7. Kd5 $1 Rxe3 8. Ra7 Rb3 9. Kc5 { with b6-b7 and Ra8+ next. Imagine the final position with the black king on f6 instead - in that case } e4 10. Rxa4 { and 10...Ke5 would be just one comfortable way to the draw, whereas here the passed e-pawn is clearly missing the support of the king. } ) 
2. h5 Rc5 ( { Or } 2... Ra1 3. g4 Rg1 4. Kf5 { - Psakhis. } ) 
3. g4 ( { Not } 3. Kf5 $2 e4 $1 { With the text Psakhis secures the h5-pawn and gains some additional space. } ) 
a5 4. Kf5 a4 { This loses a pawn, but White was ready to pick up one anyway by e3-e4 and Ke6-d6. } 5. Ra7 Rb5 6. Rxa4 Rxb2 7. Kxe5 { Rook endings with three pawns against two on one single flank are often drawn, but here the passed e-pawn and White's space advantage are telling factors. } Rg2 8. Rf4 Ra2 9. e4 Ra5+ ( { The last chance was } 9... g5 $1 10. Rf7 Kg8 11. Rb7 Rf2 { - Psakhis, although after } 12. Ke6 Rf4 13. e5 Rxg4 14. Kf6 Rf4 15. Kg6 { I suspect that Black is in big trouble anyway. } ) 
10. Ke6 Ra6+ 11. Kf5 Rf6+ 12. Ke5 Ra6 13. Rf8 $1 { A good place for the rook, where it restricts the enemy king and enhances a future advance of the e-pawn. } Ra5+ 14. Kf4 Ra1 15. Rb8 Rf1+ 16. Ke3 $1 { The e-pawn is finally ready to move forward. } Rg1 ( { If } 16... Re1 { then } 17. Kd4 Rd1 18. Kc5 { with similar play as in the game. } ) 
17. e5 $1 Re1+ ( { Or } 17... Rxg4 18. e6 Rg5 19. Kf4 Rg1 20. e7 { and wins - Psakhis. } ) 
18. Kd4 Rd1+ 19. Kc5 Rc1+ 20. Kd6 { The rest is easy. } Rd1+ 21. Ke7 Rg1 22. e6 Rxg4 23. Kd6 Rd4+ 24. Ke5 Rd1 25. e7 Re1+ 26. Kd6 Rd1+ 27. Kc5 Rc1+ 28. Kd4 
