 { This would be a typical endgame in the Carlsbad structure, was it not for the curious pawn on b4. Albeit weak, exactly that pawn caused White's downfall in the game! } 1. { -KEY- } Ra1 Ra8 { This and Black's next move are forced in order to maintain the material balance. } 2. Ra4 Rfb8 3. Rea1 $6 { A natural follow-up; } ( { but with hindsight } 3. Rc1 $1 g6 $5 { might be tried; } ( { intending } 3. Rea1 -- 4. Rxb4 Rxa5 5. Rxc6 { , was a better way of preparing the capture on b4. } ) 
 ( { At this point, } 3. Rea1 b5 4. Rxb4 Ra6 5. e4 $1 { isn't fully convincing for Black; } ) 
 { e.g. } 4. Rxb4 Rxa5 5. Rxc6 Ra2 $1 { preparing ...Re8, with some activity for the pawn. } ) 
f5 $1 { In the particular kind of position that now arises, prevention of e3-e4 will play an important role. } 4. Rxb4 $6 { Consequent, but risky. } ( { Instead, after } 4. a6 { White would have kept some initiative thanks to his sounder pawn structure. } ) 
b5 $1 { Of course! With 5.axb6 out of the question, White has to live with a rather comical rook on b4. } 5. Rc1 ( 5. e4 $1 fxe4 6. fxe4 { was a good alternative, when } Rd8 { can be met by } 7. exd5 Rxd5 8. Rc1 Ra6 9. Rc5 $1 Rxc5 ( { or } 9. Rc1 Rd6 10. d5 $1 ) 
10. dxc5 Rxa5 11. Rd4 Ra3 12. Rd6 { and a draw is in the air. } ) 
Ra6 6. Rc5 $2 { After this indifferent move, the rook is buried alive on b4. } ( { Again } 6. e4 { was called for, with similar play as in the previous note. } ) 
Re8 $1 { Preventing the the e3-e4 advance. } 7. Kf2 Re6 8. h3 Kf7 $1 { Very clever. } ( { On the obvious } 8... Rxa5 $2 { White had prepared } 9. Ra4 $1 { reverting to a normal endgame; e.g. } bxa4 10. Rxa5 axb3 11. Ra3 { etc. With the text Shengelia instead prepares to defend the c6-pawn with the king, so that the rooks can be used for active means. } ) 
9. g4 g6 10. Ke2 Ke7 11. Kd3 Kd6 12. Kd2 ( { Einarsson would have liked to activate his 'healthy' rook, but unfortunately for him, } 12. Rc2 Rxa5 ) 
 ( { or } 12. Rc1 { at any moment permits } Rxa5 ) 
Re8 13. Kd3 Re7 $1 { With the strong plan of ...Ra8-e8, which finally forces White to abandon the a-pawn. } ( { Another way to double rooks is } 13... Ra7 { heading for e7, as observed by hobbsey. In contrast, } Rxa5 $6 { allows } 14. Ra4 $1 { just as in the note to move 8, when Black would have to find } bxa4 15. Rxa5 axb3 16. Ra1 Rb8 17. Kc3 c5 $1 18. dxc5 Kxc5 19. Ra5 Kd6 20. Kb2 Re8 $1 { to take home this endgame. } ) 
14. gxf5 gxf5 15. h4 Ra8 16. Rc2 Rxa5 17. Re2 Ra1 18. e4 fxe4+ 19. fxe4 Rxe4 { A simple piece of technique, swapping the opponent's good pieces. } 20. Rxe4 dxe4+ 21. Kxe4 Rh1 
 0-1

