 { Despite the material balance White has the initiative thanks to his active rook. However, if Black manages to achieve ...g6-g5 and ...Kg6 his problems would probably be over. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 { Complicating the plan outlined in the previous note. } Rf6 $6 ( { Active defence with } 1... g5 $1 2. hxg5 Kxg5 3. Rxh7 Kg6 { was called for - Socko, who also mentions that a similar endgame was held by Black in M.Tal-M.Botvinnik, World Championship (20th matchgame), Moscow 1961. } ) 
2. Kg3 Rf5 $2 { This passive defence will soon prove fatal for Black. } ( { Again he should have resorted to } 2... g5 ) 
3. Rc7 Rf6 4. Kg4 { Zugzwang - Black has to let the enemy king get closer. } Re6 5. Kf4 Rf6+ 6. Ke5 $1 { The c6-pawn is much more valuable than the one on f3. } Rf5+ ( { Or } 6... Rxf3 7. Rxc6 Kh5 8. Rb6 Kxh4 9. c6 Rf8 10. c7 Rc8 11. Kd6 { and the d-pawn decides in the end - Socko. } ) 
7. Kd6 Rf4 8. Rxc6 Rxd4 9. Rb6 Rc4 ( { After } 9... Rxh4 10. c6 Rc4 11. c7 Rxc7 { (otherwise 12.Rc6) } 12. Kxc7 Kg5 13. Kd6 Kf4 14. Kxd5 Kxf3 15. Ke5 h5 16. Rf6 $1 ( { but not } 15... Rc4 15. Rxg6 $2 h4 { with a draw; } ) 
Kg3 17. Rxg6 { White wins - Socko. } ) 
10. Kxd5 Rc3 11. c6 Rd3+ ( { Or } 11... Kh5 12. Rb4 $1 { in the same spirit of denying enemy counterplay; e.g. } Rd3 13. Ke6 Rxf3 14. c7 Rc3 15. Kd6 Rxc7 16. Kxc7 g5 17. hxg5 Kxg5 18. Kd6 { etc. - Socko. } ) 
12. Kc4 Rxf3 13. c7 Rf4+ 14. Kd5 Rf5+ 15. Kc6 Rf6+ 16. Kb7 Rf7 17. Rb5 $1 { A final piece of restriction, cutting off the black king. As for the obvious Kb8, it can wait until the next move. } 
 1-0

