 { Here is an example with more pieces on the board.White has a slight but pleasant advantage thanks to the weak d6-pawn and the passive bishop on c8. At this point, a natural plan for him is b3-b4, a2-a4 and b4-b5, softening up the enemy queenside, but Taimanov first asks himself what might the opponent have in mind, and takes restrictive measures. } 1. { -KEY- } f4 $1 { Preventing any enemy activity associated with ...Ne5 or ...f5-f4, while fixing the f-pawns on the 'right' colour. } h6 2. b4 Re7 3. Kf2 Bd7 4. Rb1 $1 ( { The immediate } 4. a4 { would run into } c5 ) 
Kg7 5. a4 { White continues his queenside advance, and at the same time sets a little trap. } c5 $6 { Black falls right into it. Anyway, his position was quite difficult, with no active plans at hand. } 6. Ne2 $1 Bxa4 $2 ( { After } 6... b6 7. Nc3 Be6 8. Bd5 { White is also much better, but now he wins outright. } ) 
7. b5 $1 ( { With the decisive threat of } 8. Nc3 ) 
Rfe6 ( { Or } 7... axb5 8. Nc3 { followed by c4xb5 - Taimanov. } ) 
8. Nc3 axb5 9. cxb5 Rxe3 10. Nd5 $1 ( { The smartest way of grabbing material, so that fewer pieces stay on the board. In any case, } 10. Nxa4 Ra3 11. Nb6 { was not bad either. } ) 
gxf4 11. gxf4 ( { In view of } 11. gxf4 R3e6 12. Nxe7 Rxe7 13. Ra2 { Black resigned. } ) 

