 { Karpov was a true maestro in the field of restriction, and it is no wonder that he won many endgames excelling in the use of this weapon. A good case is Karpov-Illescas (in Chapter Four) , and here is another one. } 1. { -KEY- } Ra6 $1 { Restricting the enemy queenside. } ( { In contrast, the lazy } 1. Kg2 $6 { would permit } a6 $1 2. Rxb6 Rxb6 3. Bxb6 Bxb2 { with excellent drawing chances. } ) 
Kf8 ( { In the event of } 1... Rb7 2. Rbxb6 $1 Rxb6 3. Bxb6 { White wins a pawn. Nevertheless, after } Bxb2 4. Rxa7 Rxa7 5. Bxa7 { the compact pawn formations yield Black some hope for salvation - Karpov, and this possibility returns on the next move. } ) 
2. Rb4 Be5 3. Rba4 ( { The desirable } 3. b3 { permits } Bc7 $1 4. Rba4 Rb7 { and Black liberates himself to some extent. } ) 
b5 $2 { Black insists on keeping the material balance, but the passivity of his pieces will be a striking factor for the rest of the battle. } ( { The endgame a pawn down that emerges after } 3... Bxb2 4. Bxb6 Rb7 5. Bxa7 Rc8 { was a lesser evil - Karpov. } ) 
4. Ra2 Rb7 5. b3 $1 { An excellent move so as to keep pawns on both flanks. } ( { Instead, after } 5. Bxa7 $6 Bxb2 6. Rxb2 Raxa7 7. Rxa7 Rxa7 8. Rxb5 h5 $1 { Black's drawing chances again increase despite the pawn minus. } ) 
Bb8 $2 { Positional surrender. } ( { The active } 5... Rc8 { was called for, when after } 6. Rxa7 Rxa7 7. Rxa7 Rc2 8. Rb7 Rxe2 9. Rxb5 Bd6 { White's task would be more difficult than in the game. } ) 
6. Bc5 Ke8 7. d4 $1 { Karpov starts exploiting his central majority. } Kd7 8. e4 e6 9. b4 $1 { Before pushing d4-d5, it makes sense to rule out any counterplay with ...b5-b4 and ...Rb5. } Kc8 10. d5 exd5 11. exd5 Rd7 ( { The liberating attempt } 11... Be5 $2 { failed to } 12. Re2 { - Karpov. } ) 
12. d6 $1 { Further locking up Black's pieces. } Rd8 13. Kg2 { The beginning of the end: once White's king enters the battle, Black's defences will prove exhausted. } Kd7 ( { a) Seeing that } 13... Re8 { runs into the unpleasant } 14. d7 $1 Kxd7 15. Rf6 { Ribli prepares it. } ) 
 ( { b) As for } 13... Kb7 { Karpov indicates } 14. Kf3 Re8 15. Kf4 h6 16. h4 $1 { with g3-g4 and h4-h5 next, softening up the light squares for the king. } ) 
14. Re2 $1 { More restriction. } Kc8 ( { Now Black can forget about } 14... Re8 { in view of } 15. Rxe8 Kxe8 16. Kf3 { followed by Ke4-d5, with a complete paralysis of the enemy forces. } ) 
15. Re7 Rd7 16. Ra2 $1 { A decisive shift of angle. Evidently, the rook needed a very good reason to leave its restrictive post on a6, where it had stayed for 15 moves! } ( { In contrast, the greedy } 16. Rc6 $2 Kb7 17. Rxd7 Kxc6 18. Rxf7 { would have left all the previous efforts in vain after } a5 $1 ) 
a5 ( { a) White also wins after } 16... Bxd6 17. Rxd7 Kxd7 18. Rd2 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 16... Kd8 17. Bb6 $1 Kc8 18. Rc2 { - Karpov. } ) 
17. Rc2 ( { With } 18. Bb6 { coming, Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

